# c1: guidance_sigma_m
# c2: ablation
seg-d655c932abbf = Landing dispersion is the navigation error at touchdown plus an independent guidance term (<c1/>, ILLUSTRATIVE, { "20" } m per axis by default). The <c2/> table runs every combination of the aids. The ellipse is calibrated by Monte Carlo (the fraction of simulated landings inside the 3-sigma contour against the { "98.9" } % a 2-D Gaussian gives).

# text
seg-82b71e26bc89 = <b>Read it with care.</b> The default dispersion is dominated by the assumed guidance term, and at every south-polar site the relay geometry is nearly the same, so the sites differ little. Lunar time matters little through time-tagging ({ "150" } m/s times a few microseconds is under a millimetre); it matters when the geometry degrades to three relays.

# text
# c1: moonlight-service-volume
seg-f5af0aef6649 = For each site and season (an epoch and a window after it): relay positioning (four or more relays in view above the mask and the site's terrain horizon with PDOP under the threshold, from the same functions and constellation as <c1/>; the single-site result equals its figure to the bit), whether the Earth and the Sun are above the horizon (analytic Sun, Moon and lunar orientation, libration included), and timing availability (a relay or the Earth in view) with the longest stretch of neither. The relay figure does not change with the season in this model (Keplerian set, mean rotation); the Earth and Sun figures do. The phase hands on the covariance unchanged and disciplines the clock to a timing floor (ILLUSTRATIVE, { "50" } ns), leaving only the longest timing gap to hold over.

# text
seg-0890ec65a04e = The descent's five-state model on all three site axes from the surface state, an AFS fix at a stated interval with the same lunar-time treatment, and the full 6x6 position-velocity covariance at insertion handed on, with the position-velocity correlation of each axis kept. The engine cutoff dispersion moves where the vehicle is and is reported beside the navigation covariance, not carried. The errors are held in the launch site's axes (no downrange curvature) and the nominal exit state is a stand-in; only the covariance is the result.

